# How to use this file
# --------------------
# This is an example startup file. It doesn't run while its name ends in .sample.
# To use it, copy it to a name ending in .sc:
#
#     cp 10-example.sc.sample 10-project.sc
#
# A startup file holds ShellCraft commands, one per line, the same as ~/.shellcraft/rc. Every .sc
# file in this folder runs when the shell starts, right after rc, in name order: 10-… before 20-….
# They all run in your shell, so what one file sets, the next file and your prompt can use.
#
# Lines starting with # are comments, and so is a note after a command with a space before and
# after the #. A line that fails is reported with its file and line number, and the rest still run.
#
# Inside ShellCraft: `startup` lists the files, `startup run` runs them again.
# More: `man startup`, or docs/startup.md. Scripting with examples: docs/scripting/README.md.

# Ask a question; the answer is $project from here on, in this file and the ones after it.
project = input("Which project today? ")

# Set it up: modules, programs and later startup files all see these.
env set PROJECT_ROOT=~/work/$project
mkdir -p $PROJECT_ROOT
cd $PROJECT_ROOT

# Run a module or any command you'd type at the prompt.
echo "working on $project in $PROJECT_ROOT"

# Start something that keeps running, in the background (it shows in the footer).
# sub tail -f ~/work/$project/app.log
